"A failover topology shows how traffic, write authority, and dependent services move when a failure domain becomes unavailable.\n\nUse when: Review a recovery-region or standby-service design. Explain failover triggers, promotion, and failback dependencies.\n\nAvoid when: Only backup artifact restoration is being documented.\n\nVisual grammar: Padded regions identify independent failure domains. Normal and failover traffic paths have distinct labeled styles. Role-change annotations state promotion, fencing, and recovery responsibilities.\n\nBuild: Declare the failure scenario and supported recovery mode. Draw normal request and replication paths. Add failure detection, writer promotion, and alternate routing. Place failback as a separate route and reserve clear boundary-crossing corridors.\n\nCheck: Old writer fencing prevents an unexplained split-brain condition. Dependent stores, identities, and routing services support the alternate path. RPO and RTO targets are distinguished from tested results. Normal and recovery arrows do not obscure service labels.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a second region alone establishes a working recovery plan. Claiming automatic recovery when promotion still requires a manual decision.\n\nExample: A regional outage triggers a documented standby promotion and routing change after the previous writer is fenced.\n\nAlternatives: Backup and restore diagram, Replication topology."
